{{#> main title="Conclusions" }}
  <h1>Conclusions</h1>
  
  <div class="section">
    <h2>Project Summary</h2>
    <p>This project explored the application of machine learning techniques to professional Counter-Strike 2 match data, with the primary goal of predicting round winners and discovering patterns in player behavior. Through extensive experimentation with both supervised and unsupervised methods, several key insights emerged that have implications for competitive gaming analysis and machine learning practice.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Key Findings</h2>
    
    <h3>1. Economy Dominates Round Outcomes</h3>
    <p>The most significant finding across all supervised learning methods is that <strong>economic advantage is the primary predictor of round success</strong>. Equipment advantage alone accounts for 28.2% of feature importance in the best-performing model, with armor and equipment values contributing an additional 50%+ of predictive power. This quantitatively confirms what experienced players understand intuitively: teams with more money to buy superior weapons and armor have dramatically higher win probability. For competitive players, this suggests that strategic economic decisions—when to save, when to force buy, when to full buy—are more controllable and impactful than relying solely on mechanical skill execution.</p>
    
    <h3>2. Feature Engineering Matters More Than Algorithm Selection</h3>
    <p>The journey from 58.70% baseline accuracy to 86.65% final accuracy was primarily driven by feature engineering, not algorithm tuning. Switching from outcome-based features (kills, deaths) to state-based features (equipment values, player counts, health) produced a 27.95 percentage point improvement. This demonstrates that in machine learning, <strong>what is measured matters more than which algorithm is chosen</strong>. State-based features that exist before the outcome provide true predictive signal, while outcome-based features merely describe what already happened.</p>
    
    <h3>3. Time-Series Sampling Captures Game Dynamics</h3>
    <p>Sampling game state at multiple time points throughout each round (every 10 seconds) rather than once at the end provided crucial benefits: an 8.7x increase in training data (288 to 2,507 samples), capture of temporal dynamics showing how advantages develop, and training data representing various game phases. This approach enabled the boosting models to learn how early-round advantages translate into final outcomes.</p>
    
    <h3>4. Ensemble Methods Outperform Simple Models</h3>
    <p>Gradient Boosting achieved 86.65% accuracy with a ROC AUC of 0.942, dramatically outperforming individual decision trees (50-57%) and Naive Bayes classifiers (52-54%). The ensemble approach of combining many weak learners (shallow decision trees) into a strong learner proved highly effective for this prediction task. The sequential nature of boosting, where each model corrects the errors of its predecessors, captured complex interactions between economy, time, and player states.</p>
    
    <h3>5. Two Distinct Player Archetypes Exist</h3>
    <p>Unsupervised clustering analysis revealed that professional CS2 players broadly fall into two categories: <strong>Entry Fraggers</strong> (aggressive, high kills, more deaths, opening kills) and <strong>Support/Finishing Players</strong> (conservative, higher survival, post-plant kills). PCA analysis showed that 70.3% of player behavior variance can be captured in just two dimensions representing overall activity level and playstyle aggressiveness. This finding could inform team composition strategies and player role optimization.</p>
  </div>

