  <div class="section">
    <h2>Implications for Competitive Gaming</h2>
    <p>The findings from this analysis have practical implications for competitive CS2 players and teams:</p>
    
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Prioritize Economy:</strong> Strategic saving, forcing opponent eco rounds, and preserving weapons should be prioritized as the most controllable factor influencing match outcomes</li>
      <li><strong>Team Composition:</strong> Understanding the Entry Fragger vs Support dichotomy can inform roster building and role assignment</li>
      <li><strong>Mid-Round Decisions:</strong> The high importance of player count advantage and health totals suggests that trading kills efficiently and avoiding unnecessary deaths are crucial</li>
      <li><strong>Armor Investment:</strong> Armor metrics showed surprisingly high importance (CT: 19.8%, T: 10.2%), suggesting armor purchases may be undervalued in some strategies</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Lessons for Machine Learning Practice</h2>
    <p>Beyond the domain-specific findings, this project reinforced several important machine learning principles:</p>
    
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Data Quality Over Algorithm Complexity:</strong> Proper feature engineering produced larger gains than algorithm selection or hyperparameter tuning</li>
      <li><strong>Beware of Data Leakage:</strong> Using outcome-based features to predict outcomes is a subtle but critical error that produces meaningless models</li>
      <li><strong>Match Algorithm to Data:</strong> Naive Bayes assumptions were violated by correlated game features; algorithm selection must consider data characteristics</li>
      <li><strong>Ensemble Methods Provide Robustness:</strong> Combining weak learners through boosting provided both accuracy and resistance to overfitting</li>
      <li><strong>Validation Strategy Matters:</strong> Group-aware cross-validation was essential when using time-series sampling to prevent information leakage</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
    <p>This project demonstrated that machine learning can effectively predict Counter-Strike 2 round outcomes with 86.65% accuracy when provided with appropriate state-based features. The dominance of economic factors in the predictions quantitatively confirms strategic principles that experienced players have developed through intuition. The journey from 50% (random guessing) to 86.65% accuracy illustrates the importance of understanding the problem domain, engineering meaningful features, and selecting appropriate algorithms for the data characteristics.</p>
    
    <p>Future work could extend this analysis to incorporate positional data, utility usage, and player-specific models. Real-time prediction during live matches represents an exciting application possibility, potentially providing strategic insights for coaches and analysts. The techniques developed here could also be adapted to other competitive games with similar structured data availability.</p>
    
    <p>Ultimately, this project showcases how machine learning can extract actionable insights from competitive gaming data, bridging the gap between intuitive player knowledge and quantitative analysis.</p>
  </div>

